1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,840
Let me start with something
that happened at BrightLeaf.

2
00:00:03,101 --> 00:00:06,914
Last month they ordered 200 rolls
of stretch wrap they didn't actually

3
00:00:06,954 --> 00:00:11,573
need. That's about $1,840 worth
of film sitting in the Columbus

4
00:00:11,613 --> 00:00:15,320
warehouse. And the strange thing
is, the spreadsheet looked perfectly

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:19,901
reasonable. The stock sheet showed
21 rolls of stretch wrap, SKU

6
00:00:19,941 --> 00:00:24,730
BL-1003. The reorder point was 200,
so the system flagged it as low and

7
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:26,197
a purchase order went out.

8
00:00:26,237 --> 00:00:27,507
But there was a problem.

9
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:31,265
The warehouse team had not counted
individual rolls, they had counted

10
00:00:31,305 --> 00:00:35,940
cartons. Each carton had 12 rolls
and there were 8 loose rolls as well.

11
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:41,302
So the actual stock was not 21
rolls, it was 21 cartons of 12 plus 8

12
00:00:41,342 --> 00:00:44,242
loose rolls. That's 260 rolls in total.

13
00:00:44,282 --> 00:00:48,229
They had plenty of stock, the number
in the sheet was not exactly wrong.

14
00:00:48,399 --> 00:00:49,649
The unit was missing.

15
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:54,097
Now, think about what happens when
an AI agent reads data like this.

16
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:57,287
It does not stop and ask
someone what the number means.

17
00:00:57,327 --> 00:01:00,827
It might read that sheet every
hour and raise a purchase order in

18
00:01:00,867 --> 00:01:04,268
seconds. So if the data is messy,
the agent can make the wrong decision

19
00:01:04,308 --> 00:01:06,338
very confidently and very quickly.

20
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,638
That's why we are spending a whole
video on databases and data quality.

21
00:01:10,678 --> 00:01:13,882
And the good news is, this is
not as technical as it sounds.

22
00:01:13,922 --> 00:01:17,432
If you have worked with Excel, you
already understand most of the basic

23
00:01:17,482 --> 00:01:20,739
ideas. We will use Google Sheets
to build two tables for Stocky:

24
00:01:20,779 --> 00:01:23,289
One for suppliers and
one for the Stock Log.

25
00:01:23,421 --> 00:01:27,514
I'll also show you five simple
rules that make data much easier and

26
00:01:27,554 --> 00:01:29,251
safer for an agent to work with.

27
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:33,053
Then, we will take one of BrightLeaf's
messy stock exports and clean it

28
00:01:33,093 --> 00:01:37,808
up together. It has merged cells,
duplicate entries, mixed units,

29
00:01:38,018 --> 00:01:40,468
and even quantities
like approximately 300.

30
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:43,200
We will fix those issues step by step.

31
00:01:43,381 --> 00:01:47,941
No coding, no SQL, and nothing to
install, just a kind of spreadsheet

32
00:01:47,981 --> 00:01:50,631
work many of you have
probably dealt with before.

33
00:01:50,802 --> 00:01:53,032
Alright, let's open the filing cabinet.

34
00:01:54,053 --> 00:01:58,513
Let's start somewhere familiar,
your BrightLeaf Odoo from Video 2.

35
00:01:58,553 --> 00:02:02,533
I'm in Inventory, Products, and I'm
switching from the picture cards to

36
00:02:02,573 --> 00:02:06,083
the list view, the icon with the
little lines on the top right.

37
00:02:06,407 --> 00:02:08,787
Look at this. Rows and columns.

38
00:02:08,827 --> 00:02:10,547
Each row is one product.

39
00:02:10,587 --> 00:02:14,327
Each column is one piece of
information about that product: the

40
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:18,513
reference, the name, the sales price,
the cost, the quantity on hand.

41
00:02:18,853 --> 00:02:22,453
Guess what? Underneath, Odoo
stores this in a database.

42
00:02:22,493 --> 00:02:26,513
And a database, at the level we need,
is simply a super-organised Excel

43
00:02:26,553 --> 00:02:30,117
workbook. Four words, and
you know all of them already.

44
00:02:30,393 --> 00:02:33,093
A table is like one sheet in a workbook.

45
00:02:33,133 --> 00:02:36,933
Odoo has a table for products, a
table for suppliers, a table for

46
00:02:36,973 --> 00:02:40,627
purchase orders. A row
is one record, one thing.

47
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:42,667
One product. One supplier.

48
00:02:42,707 --> 00:02:47,321
One stock count. A column is one
field, one type of information that

49
00:02:47,361 --> 00:02:50,061
every record has. Product name.

50
00:02:50,101 --> 00:02:54,115
Cost. Lead time. And the database
is the whole workbook holding all of

51
00:02:54,155 --> 00:02:55,305
those tables together.

52
00:02:55,541 --> 00:02:58,061
Now here's something
most people never notice.

53
00:02:58,101 --> 00:02:59,961
I'll open the pallet jack wheels.

54
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:01,921
Look at the very end of the web address.

55
00:03:01,961 --> 00:03:06,121
There's a number. That number is
Odoo's own internal ID for this

56
00:03:06,161 --> 00:03:08,481
product. It never changes.

57
00:03:08,521 --> 00:03:12,281
You can rename the product, change
the price, change the supplier,

58
00:03:12,321 --> 00:03:14,341
and that number stays the same.

59
00:03:14,381 --> 00:03:18,241
Hold on to that idea, because unique
IDs are going to save us in a few

60
00:03:18,281 --> 00:03:22,363
minutes. So why are we using Google
Sheets as Stocky's database instead

61
00:03:22,403 --> 00:03:23,263
of something fancy?

62
00:03:23,303 --> 00:03:28,157
Three reasons. One, it's free and
you already know how to use it.

63
00:03:28,197 --> 00:03:33,477
Two, it lives online, so our
automation tool, n8n, can reach it from

64
00:03:33,517 --> 00:03:37,317
anywhere, whereas an Excel file
sitting on your laptop is locked inside

65
00:03:37,357 --> 00:03:41,837
your laptop. And three, you can
open it and see exactly what Stocky

66
00:03:41,877 --> 00:03:44,407
wrote. Nothing is hidden in a black box.

67
00:03:44,699 --> 00:03:48,439
There's an app called Airtable that
does the same job with a few more

68
00:03:48,479 --> 00:03:50,779
features, and it's a fine alternative.

69
00:03:50,819 --> 00:03:53,289
But for learning, Google
Sheets is perfect.

70
00:03:53,681 --> 00:03:57,241
My shortcut: I just typed "sheets
dot new" in the address bar,

71
00:03:57,281 --> 00:03:59,521
and a blank sheet opened instantly.

72
00:03:59,561 --> 00:04:03,641
I'll click the title "Untitled
spreadsheet" and rename it "BrightLeaf

73
00:04:03,681 --> 00:04:04,831
Stocky Database".

74
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:09,100
Before we start building our tables,
there's one important thing I want

75
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:11,982
to explain. Why do we need unique IDs?

76
00:04:12,022 --> 00:04:14,082
Let me give an example from BrightLeaf.

77
00:04:14,277 --> 00:04:17,107
Mr. Harrison once asked
Sarah a simple question.

78
00:04:17,569 --> 00:04:20,371
How much are we spending
with PackRight Industries?

79
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:24,162
I want to see if we can
negotiate a volume discount.

80
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:30,253
Sarah went into the purchase history,
exported the data to excel and

81
00:04:30,293 --> 00:04:31,979
searched for PackRight Industries.

82
00:04:32,019 --> 00:04:36,359
The results showed that they accounted
for about 18% of Brightleaf's

83
00:04:36,399 --> 00:04:39,844
packaging spend. That did not look
like enough to negotiate a meaningful

84
00:04:39,884 --> 00:04:42,430
discount. So Mr. Harrison
dropped the idea.

85
00:04:42,647 --> 00:04:45,553
But six months later,
Sarah discovered something.

86
00:04:45,593 --> 00:04:48,643
PackRight was actually listed
three different ways in the system.

87
00:04:48,767 --> 00:04:51,962
There was PackRight
Industries, Pack Right Ind.

88
00:04:52,002 --> 00:04:55,899
with the space and PACKRIGHT
(Chicago) in capital letters.

89
00:04:55,939 --> 00:04:59,327
They were all the same suppliers,
but because the names were different,

90
00:04:59,367 --> 00:05:02,317
the spreadsheet treated them
as three separate companies.

91
00:05:02,407 --> 00:05:07,087
Once Sarah combined those records,
PackRight actually represented 41% of

92
00:05:07,127 --> 00:05:08,225
the packaging spend.

93
00:05:08,265 --> 00:05:09,495
That's a big difference.

94
00:05:10,005 --> 00:05:13,215
BrightLeaf had much more negotiating
power than they realised.

95
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:16,410
And this is why relying on
names can cause problems.

96
00:05:16,792 --> 00:05:20,981
People type names differently,
companies changes their name and

97
00:05:21,211 --> 00:05:24,343
sometimes two completely different
businesses have very similar name.

98
00:05:24,383 --> 00:05:26,213
A unique ID solve that problem.

99
00:05:26,253 --> 00:05:30,413
Think of it like a passport number,
loads of people can be called Sarah

100
00:05:30,453 --> 00:05:33,786
Mitchell but each person has
a different passport number.

101
00:05:33,891 --> 00:05:38,331
So in your database PackRight
gets one ID: SUP-002.

102
00:05:38,584 --> 00:05:42,203
Every purchase order or stock records
that refers to PackRight uses that

103
00:05:42,243 --> 00:05:46,532
same ID. The name might change or be
entered differently somewhere else,

104
00:05:46,572 --> 00:05:50,503
but the ID stays the same and this
is especially important when an AI

105
00:05:50,543 --> 00:05:52,153
agent is working with your data.

106
00:05:52,302 --> 00:05:55,821
We don't want it guessing which
supplier a record refers to.

107
00:05:55,861 --> 00:06:00,021
Give a record a clear unique ID and
the agent has something reliable to

108
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:00,751
work with.

109
00:06:01,673 --> 00:06:04,073
Let's build Stocky's first table.

110
00:06:04,113 --> 00:06:06,473
One table for one kind of thing.

111
00:06:06,513 --> 00:06:10,053
This table is about
suppliers, and only suppliers.

112
00:06:10,093 --> 00:06:13,403
I double-click the tab at the
bottom and rename it "Suppliers".

113
00:06:14,003 --> 00:06:17,163
Now the headers, the
column names, in row 1.

114
00:06:17,203 --> 00:06:22,323
Supplier ID, supplier name, city,
country code, currency, latitude,

115
00:06:22,363 --> 00:06:25,013
longitude, contact email, and active.

116
00:06:25,337 --> 00:06:27,837
Notice two things about
how I'm writing them.

117
00:06:27,877 --> 00:06:30,477
First, there's exactly one header row.

118
00:06:30,517 --> 00:06:34,657
No title above it, no "BrightLeaf
supplier list, updated by Sarah" in a

119
00:06:34,697 --> 00:06:39,597
merged banner. Row 1 is headers,
and data starts in row 2.

120
00:06:39,637 --> 00:06:41,947
Agents and automation tools expect that.

121
00:06:42,415 --> 00:06:46,775
Second, I'm using underscores
instead of spaces, like "Supplier

122
00:06:46,815 --> 00:06:51,455
underscore ID". Spaces in headers
work in Sheets, but they cause small

123
00:06:51,495 --> 00:06:54,055
annoyances in automation tools later.

124
00:06:54,095 --> 00:06:55,855
Underscores never do.

125
00:06:55,895 --> 00:06:58,745
It's a two-second habit
that saves real headaches.

126
00:06:58,977 --> 00:07:03,117
I'll make the headers bold,
then View, Freeze, one row.

127
00:07:03,157 --> 00:07:06,067
Now when the list gets long,
the headers stay on screen.

128
00:07:06,331 --> 00:07:11,871
Now the data. Supplier
ID first, SUP dash 001.

129
00:07:11,911 --> 00:07:14,111
Apex Fasteners, Columbus.

130
00:07:14,151 --> 00:07:18,251
SUP-002, PackRight Industries, Chicago.

131
00:07:18,291 --> 00:07:22,121
All the way to SUP-006,
Shenzhen BrightTech.

132
00:07:22,729 --> 00:07:24,229
See the ID pattern?

133
00:07:24,269 --> 00:07:27,449
Three letters that tell you what
kind of thing it is, a dash,

134
00:07:27,489 --> 00:07:29,649
and a number with leading zeros.

135
00:07:29,689 --> 00:07:34,729
SUP for suppliers, and in a
minute LOG for stock log entries.

136
00:07:34,769 --> 00:07:40,009
When you see "SUP-004" in an email,
you instantly know it's a supplier,

137
00:07:40,049 --> 00:07:44,011
not a product. Now look at the
latitude and longitude columns.

138
00:07:44,051 --> 00:07:45,831
Recognise those numbers?

139
00:07:45,871 --> 00:07:52,251
22.54 and 114.06. That's
Shenzhen, the exact numbers we

140
00:07:52,291 --> 00:07:54,931
sent to the weather API in the last video.

141
00:07:54,971 --> 00:07:57,071
This is where it all connects.

142
00:07:57,111 --> 00:08:01,131
Later, Stocky will read this table,
take each supplier's latitude and

143
00:08:01,171 --> 00:08:04,331
longitude, and build
its own weather request.

144
00:08:04,371 --> 00:08:08,351
Same with the currency column:
it feeds the currency API.

145
00:08:08,391 --> 00:08:10,881
Your database becomes
the agent's to-do list.

146
00:08:11,241 --> 00:08:15,581
Pause and think. Same city and
coordinates, but different supplier IDs.

147
00:08:15,621 --> 00:08:16,071
Why?

148
00:08:21,411 --> 00:08:23,051
Because they're different companies.

149
00:08:23,091 --> 00:08:27,331
Same city, same weather, but different
contracts, different products,

150
00:08:27,371 --> 00:08:28,831
different people to call.

151
00:08:28,871 --> 00:08:31,761
The ID identifies the
supplier, not the city.

152
00:08:32,037 --> 00:08:34,977
Here's a trick that prevents
mess before it happens.

153
00:08:35,017 --> 00:08:39,637
I select the country code column,
then Data, Data validation,

154
00:08:39,677 --> 00:08:44,277
Add rule. For criteria, I choose
"Dropdown", and I type the allowed

155
00:08:44,317 --> 00:08:48,477
values: US, MX, CN, AE.

156
00:08:48,517 --> 00:08:53,723
Done. Now nobody can type "Mexico"
in one row, "MEX" in another and "mx"

157
00:08:53,763 --> 00:08:56,443
in a third. They pick from the list.

158
00:08:56,483 --> 00:08:59,703
A dropdown is a tiny
guard dog for your data.

159
00:08:59,743 --> 00:09:04,993
I'll do the same for the currency
column: USD, MXN, CNY, AED.

160
00:09:05,689 --> 00:09:07,749
Last one. The "Active" column.

161
00:09:07,789 --> 00:09:10,869
I select it and click Insert, Checkbox.

162
00:09:10,909 --> 00:09:12,729
TRUE becomes a ticked box.

163
00:09:12,769 --> 00:09:16,429
If BrightLeaf stops buying from
a supplier, Sarah unticks it.

164
00:09:16,469 --> 00:09:20,009
We don't delete the row, because old
purchase orders still point to that

165
00:09:20,049 --> 00:09:23,799
ID. We just mark it inactive,
and Stocky will skip it.

166
00:09:25,667 --> 00:09:27,787
Second table: the Stock Log.

167
00:09:27,827 --> 00:09:31,487
This is where Stocky will write
down every stock check it does.

168
00:09:31,527 --> 00:09:35,987
I click the plus sign at the bottom,
rename the tab "Stock underscore

169
00:09:36,027 --> 00:09:42,127
Log", and add the headers: log
ID, log date, SKU, quantity on

170
00:09:42,167 --> 00:09:48,007
hand, unit, reorder point,
status, supplier ID, logged by,

171
00:09:48,047 --> 00:09:50,677
and note. Bold and freeze.

172
00:09:51,089 --> 00:09:54,049
A log is like a ship's logbook or a diary.

173
00:09:54,089 --> 00:09:56,369
You never rub out yesterday's entry.

174
00:09:56,409 --> 00:09:58,569
Every check gets a brand-new row.

175
00:09:58,609 --> 00:10:02,569
That way, three months from now,
Sarah can ask, "How fast were we

176
00:10:02,609 --> 00:10:04,749
burning through safety gloves in October?"

177
00:10:04,789 --> 00:10:06,579
and the answer is sitting right there.

178
00:10:06,827 --> 00:10:08,707
Let me add three entries.

179
00:10:08,747 --> 00:10:14,487
LOG-0001, today's date,
BL-1005, the pallet jack

180
00:10:14,527 --> 00:10:17,347
wheels. Quantity: 18.

181
00:10:17,387 --> 00:10:21,447
Unit: set. Reorder point: 25.

182
00:10:21,487 --> 00:10:25,787
Supplier: SUP-004. Logged by Sarah.

183
00:10:25,827 --> 00:10:29,697
And in the note column: "3
pallet jacks waiting for wheels."

184
00:10:29,937 --> 00:10:32,397
Now pay close attention
to the quantity cell.

185
00:10:32,437 --> 00:10:35,357
I typed "18". Just the number.

186
00:10:35,397 --> 00:10:38,037
Not "18 sets". Not "18 urgent!".

187
00:10:38,077 --> 00:10:40,577
urgent!". The unit has its own column.

188
00:10:40,617 --> 00:10:42,557
The comment has its own column.

189
00:10:42,597 --> 00:10:46,927
This is the most important rule in
this whole video: one fact per cell.

190
00:10:47,307 --> 00:10:49,447
Why? Because "18" is a number.

191
00:10:49,487 --> 00:10:53,927
You can add it, compare it,
and ask "is it below 25?".

192
00:10:53,967 --> 00:10:55,347
But "18 sets urgent!"

193
00:10:55,387 --> 00:10:57,107
urgent!" is just text.

194
00:10:57,147 --> 00:11:00,967
A formula can't do maths with it,
and an agent has to guess what you

195
00:11:01,007 --> 00:11:03,621
meant. That brings us to data types.

196
00:11:03,661 --> 00:11:06,081
Every column holds one type of thing.

197
00:11:06,121 --> 00:11:07,561
Quantity holds numbers.

198
00:11:07,601 --> 00:11:11,141
Names hold text. Active
holds true or false.

199
00:11:11,181 --> 00:11:14,571
And dates hold real dates, not
text that looks like a date.

200
00:11:14,839 --> 00:11:19,539
I select the date column, then
Format, Number, Custom date and time,

201
00:11:19,579 --> 00:11:22,079
and pick year-month-day.

202
00:11:22,119 --> 00:11:27,479
Why? Because "03/04" means the 3rd
of April in Mexico, where SafeHands

203
00:11:27,519 --> 00:11:30,039
is, and March the 4th here in the US.

204
00:11:30,079 --> 00:11:36,079
2026-11-16 can't be misread by
anyone, human or machine, and it sorts

205
00:11:36,119 --> 00:11:39,349
correctly. Now let's make
the table do some work.

206
00:11:39,389 --> 00:11:45,317
In the status column I type: equals
IF, open bracket, D2 less than or

207
00:11:45,357 --> 00:11:50,827
equal to F2, comma, "REORDER" in
quotes, comma, "OK" in quotes,

208
00:11:50,867 --> 00:11:55,209
close bracket. In English: if
quantity on hand is at or below the

209
00:11:55,249 --> 00:11:59,511
reorder point, say
REORDER, otherwise say OK.

210
00:11:59,871 --> 00:12:05,591
I drag it down. Pallet jack
wheels, 18 against 25: REORDER.

211
00:12:05,631 --> 00:12:11,991
Corrugated boxes, 1,180
against 1,500: REORDER.

212
00:12:12,031 --> 00:12:16,041
Hex bolts, 145 against 80: OK.

213
00:12:16,169 --> 00:12:20,209
That formula only works because
quantity and reorder point are clean

214
00:12:20,249 --> 00:12:24,949
numbers. If D2 said "18
sets", this would break.

215
00:12:24,989 --> 00:12:27,739
Clean data is what makes
the smart stuff possible.

216
00:12:28,043 --> 00:12:31,283
One last bit of magic, for
my fellow Excel lovers.

217
00:12:31,323 --> 00:12:34,923
The stock log only stores the
supplier ID, not the name.

218
00:12:34,963 --> 00:12:37,983
But I can look up the name
from the Suppliers table.

219
00:12:38,023 --> 00:12:43,983
In K2: equals XLOOKUP, H2, then
the Suppliers ID column, then the

220
00:12:44,023 --> 00:12:45,603
Suppliers name column.

221
00:12:45,643 --> 00:12:47,421
Drag down. There they are.

222
00:12:47,781 --> 00:12:49,941
This is what makes it a real database.

223
00:12:49,981 --> 00:12:52,141
The name lives in one place only.

224
00:12:52,181 --> 00:12:56,321
If PackRight renames itself tomorrow,
Sarah changes it once, and every

225
00:12:56,361 --> 00:12:58,821
stock log row updates instantly.

226
00:12:58,861 --> 00:13:02,121
In database language, this ID
connection is called a relationship.

227
00:13:02,161 --> 00:13:03,927
You've just built one.

228
00:13:04,556 --> 00:13:07,306
We have now built the Suppliers
table and the stock log.

229
00:13:07,526 --> 00:13:11,206
Before we move on, let's step back
and look what actually make these

230
00:13:11,246 --> 00:13:13,365
tables easy for an agent to work with.

231
00:13:13,405 --> 00:13:14,926
There are five simple rules.

232
00:13:14,966 --> 00:13:18,126
These are worth remembering because
they apply to almost any business

233
00:13:18,166 --> 00:13:21,407
spreadsheets, not just the one
we are building for Stocky.

234
00:13:21,447 --> 00:13:24,320
The first rule, one table
for one kind of thing.

235
00:13:24,567 --> 00:13:28,127
Keep supplier information in the
Suppliers table and stock checks in the

236
00:13:28,167 --> 00:13:31,097
stock log. Don't mix everything
into one giant sheet.

237
00:13:31,196 --> 00:13:34,922
The second rule, keep just one
header row right at the top.

238
00:13:34,962 --> 00:13:39,111
No big title above it, no blank
rows and no extra notes before the

239
00:13:39,151 --> 00:13:40,213
actual column names.

240
00:13:40,253 --> 00:13:42,383
Your data should start on row 2.

241
00:13:42,667 --> 00:13:44,717
Third, one fact per cell.

242
00:13:44,887 --> 00:13:50,190
For example, don't put 18 sets
urgent into one cell, put 18 in the

243
00:13:50,230 --> 00:13:54,473
quantity column, set in the unit
column and urgent in the note column.

244
00:13:54,647 --> 00:13:58,897
That way both formulas and agents
can understand what each value means.

245
00:13:59,005 --> 00:14:01,457
Fourth, Give every row a unique ID.

246
00:14:01,497 --> 00:14:04,767
As you saw with PackRight,
names can be inconsistent.

247
00:14:04,901 --> 00:14:11,111
An ID like SUP-002 or LOG-0001
gives each record a reliable

248
00:14:11,221 --> 00:14:14,919
reference. And fifth, Keep your
data types and units consistent.

249
00:14:15,133 --> 00:14:18,677
Dates should be actual dates in one
clear format, quantities should be

250
00:14:18,717 --> 00:14:22,297
number, and the units should be
recorded separately, not left for

251
00:14:22,337 --> 00:14:26,094
someone to guess. That's one
extra thing I'd add here.

252
00:14:26,134 --> 00:14:29,994
Avoid merged cells and don't use
colors as the only way to communicate

253
00:14:30,034 --> 00:14:33,828
meaning. A red cell might mean
urgent to you, but an agent doesn't

254
00:14:33,968 --> 00:14:37,681
automatically understand that, put
the word urgent in a remark or status

255
00:14:37,721 --> 00:14:41,972
field instead. So, those are the
five rules, they sound simple but

256
00:14:42,012 --> 00:14:45,252
following them makes a huge difference
to how reliable your systems and

257
00:14:45,292 --> 00:14:46,478
agents can use the data.

258
00:14:46,518 --> 00:14:50,678
Now, think about the spreadsheet
you use most often at work, which of

259
00:14:50,718 --> 00:14:52,228
these rules does it break?

260
00:14:52,378 --> 00:14:55,407
Pause the video for a moment
and note down the biggest issue,

261
00:14:55,447 --> 00:14:57,857
you will get a chance to
fix it in the activity.

262
00:15:06,867 --> 00:15:10,827
Now the fun part. This is
BrightLeaf's real-world stock sheet.

263
00:15:10,867 --> 00:15:14,787
It started as an Odoo export three
years ago and has been passed between

264
00:15:14,827 --> 00:15:18,397
five people since. You might
recognise some of these crimes.

265
00:15:18,813 --> 00:15:23,153
Merged title rows. Supplier
names merged across several rows.

266
00:15:23,193 --> 00:15:29,103
Quantities like "145
boxes", "approx 1,850"

267
00:15:29,353 --> 00:15:31,513
and "18 urgent!!". urgent!!".

268
00:15:31,553 --> 00:15:36,833
"BL 1002" with a space next
to "BL-1002" with a dash.

269
00:15:36,873 --> 00:15:40,953
A blank SKU. Dates in
five different formats.

270
00:15:40,993 --> 00:15:43,373
Red cells only Tom understands.

271
00:15:43,413 --> 00:15:46,453
And a TOTAL row that just says "lots".

272
00:15:46,493 --> 00:15:48,193
A human can muddle through.

273
00:15:48,233 --> 00:15:50,333
An agent will stumble on every one.

274
00:15:50,373 --> 00:15:55,627
Let's fix them. Fix one: I delete
the top three rows: the merged title,

275
00:15:55,667 --> 00:15:58,327
the "prepared by" line and the blank row.

276
00:15:58,367 --> 00:16:00,017
Now row 1 is the headers.

277
00:16:00,509 --> 00:16:02,789
Fix two: merged cells.

278
00:16:02,829 --> 00:16:06,589
The supplier column looks tidy,
but only the top cell of a merge

279
00:16:06,629 --> 00:16:08,229
actually holds the name.

280
00:16:08,269 --> 00:16:12,289
The rows underneath are empty, so
if you sort or filter, products lose

281
00:16:12,329 --> 00:16:16,649
their supplier. Format,
Merge cells, Unmerge.

282
00:16:16,689 --> 00:16:19,809
See the blanks? I copy each name down.

283
00:16:19,849 --> 00:16:21,999
Every row now knows its own supplier.

284
00:16:22,127 --> 00:16:24,987
Fix three: inconsistent IDs.

285
00:16:25,027 --> 00:16:31,107
To a computer, "BL 1002" and
"BL-1002" are as different as

286
00:16:31,147 --> 00:16:32,927
"apple" and "banana".

287
00:16:32,967 --> 00:16:38,627
Edit, Find and replace, "BL space"
with "BL dash", Replace all.

288
00:16:38,667 --> 00:16:43,967
Then Data, Data cleanup, Trim
whitespace, to remove invisible spaces,

289
00:16:44,007 --> 00:16:48,347
the number one reason a VLOOKUP says
"not found" when you can see the

290
00:16:48,387 --> 00:16:51,713
value right there. Fix four: duplicates.

291
00:16:51,753 --> 00:16:55,553
Data, Data cleanup, Remove
duplicates, data has a header row.

292
00:16:55,593 --> 00:16:57,813
The duplicate hex bolts disappear.

293
00:16:57,853 --> 00:17:01,673
The two corrugated box rows had
different date formats, so I delete one

294
00:17:01,713 --> 00:17:07,373
by hand. Before this, a stock total
would have counted 2,360 boxes

295
00:17:07,413 --> 00:17:13,473
instead of 1,180, which is above
the reorder point of 1,500, so the

296
00:17:13,513 --> 00:17:15,563
reorder alert would never have fired.

297
00:17:15,927 --> 00:17:18,427
Fix five: one fact per cell.

298
00:17:18,467 --> 00:17:22,707
I insert a Unit column
and split every quantity.

299
00:17:22,747 --> 00:17:27,527
"145 boxes" becomes 145 and "box".

300
00:17:27,567 --> 00:17:33,547
"approx 1,850" becomes 1,850, with

301
00:17:33,587 --> 00:17:35,967
"estimate, recount" in the remarks.

302
00:17:36,007 --> 00:17:42,467
And our stretch wrap: "21 cartons,
12 rolls each, plus 8 loose" becomes

303
00:17:42,507 --> 00:17:45,987
260 rolls, because the
reorder point is in rolls.

304
00:17:46,027 --> 00:17:47,787
Not a stock-out. The

305
00:17:47,827 --> 00:17:50,207
eighteen-hundred-dollar
cell, fixed in five

306
00:17:50,247 --> 00:17:53,385
seconds. Fix six: the blank ID.

307
00:17:53,425 --> 00:17:56,025
The safety gloves have no SKU.

308
00:17:56,065 --> 00:17:59,965
The product list says BL-1004.

309
00:18:00,005 --> 00:18:03,055
A row without an ID is a
parcel without a label.

310
00:18:03,443 --> 00:18:07,603
Fix seven: the red cells meant
"urgent", so I write that in the remarks

311
00:18:07,643 --> 00:18:09,223
and reset the colour.

312
00:18:09,263 --> 00:18:11,143
And the TOTAL row goes.

313
00:18:11,183 --> 00:18:15,157
Totals belong in reports, not data
tables, or the agent counts them as a

314
00:18:15,197 --> 00:18:17,741
product. Before and after.

315
00:18:17,781 --> 00:18:21,141
Messy: seventeen rows and
two red "urgent" cells.

316
00:18:21,181 --> 00:18:26,521
Clean: ten rows, one per product,
and four need reordering: boxes,

317
00:18:26,561 --> 00:18:29,001
gloves, wheels and hydraulic oil.

318
00:18:29,041 --> 00:18:33,421
Two surprises. The packing
tape coloured red for "urgent"?

319
00:18:33,461 --> 00:18:37,841
1,850 rolls against a
reorder point of 1,000.

320
00:18:37,881 --> 00:18:43,141
Fine. And the hydraulic oil nobody
flagged is at 31 drums against 40,

321
00:18:43,181 --> 00:18:45,981
with a 14-day lead time from Dubai.

322
00:18:46,021 --> 00:18:50,001
That's a real emergency, and the
messy sheet was hiding it under "check

323
00:18:50,041 --> 00:18:50,715
with Tom".

324
00:18:51,567 --> 00:18:55,247
Before we wrap up, there are three
common mistakes I want you to avoid

325
00:18:55,287 --> 00:18:56,817
when you are setting up a database.

326
00:18:57,022 --> 00:19:00,502
The first one is cleaning your data
once and assuming the problem is

327
00:19:00,542 --> 00:19:04,092
solved. If people can still
enter dates in different formats,

328
00:19:04,302 --> 00:19:07,892
type any country name they want
or mix units in the same cell,

329
00:19:07,987 --> 00:19:09,357
the mess will come back.

330
00:19:09,712 --> 00:19:13,890
That's why we added drop-downs and
check boxes earlier, try to prevent

331
00:19:14,140 --> 00:19:16,770
inconsistent data at the
point where it's entered.

332
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:20,470
The second mistake is trying to
make the database look like a

333
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:24,488
presentation. I know, it's tempting
to add merged headings, colors,

334
00:19:24,798 --> 00:19:27,598
logos and blank rows to
make everything look nice.

335
00:19:27,638 --> 00:19:29,768
But a database is not a report.

336
00:19:29,808 --> 00:19:33,048
It needs to be predictable
and easy for software to read.

337
00:19:33,088 --> 00:19:35,908
Keep the visual styling for
your reports and dashboards.

338
00:19:36,018 --> 00:19:39,898
and third mistake is putting
absolutely everything into one giant

339
00:19:39,938 --> 00:19:43,851
spreadsheet supplier details,
product information, stock levels,

340
00:19:43,997 --> 00:19:46,927
purchase orders all in
one sheet with 40 columns.

341
00:19:47,102 --> 00:19:50,899
That might seem convenient at first
but it become difficult to maintain.

342
00:19:51,092 --> 00:19:54,692
Keep separate tables for separate
things and connect them using unique

343
00:19:54,732 --> 00:19:58,457
IDs. If you avoid three mistakes
you will already have much cleaner

344
00:19:58,497 --> 00:20:00,427
foundation for the agents you build later.

345
00:20:00,567 --> 00:20:03,017
All right, let's bring
everything together.

346
00:20:03,175 --> 00:20:07,145
There are three main ideas from this
video that I want you to remember.

347
00:20:07,384 --> 00:20:11,194
First, a database is not some
mysterious technical system.

348
00:20:11,303 --> 00:20:14,823
At the level we have covered today,
you can think of it as a well

349
00:20:14,863 --> 00:20:15,883
organized workbook.

350
00:20:15,997 --> 00:20:20,549
Tables are like sheets, rows are
records and columns are fields.

351
00:20:20,702 --> 00:20:24,152
That's why we're using Google
Sheet to get Stocky started.

352
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:28,681
Second, every record needs a unique
ID, names can be misspelled or

353
00:20:28,721 --> 00:20:32,336
entered in different ways, but an
ID gives us one reliable way to

354
00:20:32,376 --> 00:20:35,651
identify a supplier, a
product or a stock log entry.

355
00:20:35,691 --> 00:20:38,281
It's also how our tables
connect to each other.

356
00:20:38,418 --> 00:20:42,876
and third the quality of your data
matters keep one table for each kind

357
00:20:42,916 --> 00:20:47,772
of thing use one header row put one
fact in each cell give every row a

358
00:20:47,812 --> 00:20:52,335
unique ID and keep your data types
and units consistent avoid merge

359
00:20:52,375 --> 00:20:56,146
cells and don't rely on colors to
communicate information now it's your

360
00:20:56,186 --> 00:21:00,753
turn to put this in your practice
Download the V04 Activity: messy data

361
